WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.487
The fields you use for duplicate detection change the meaning of the result.

00:00:04.487 --> 00:00:12.417
Using normalized email alone is simple and often useful, but people can change emails, share accounts,

00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:14.512
or have missing values.

00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:21.182
Using name plus email is more specific, but differences in spelling or missing information can split records that

00:00:21.182 --> 00:00:25.352
actually belong to the same person.

00:00:25.352 --> 00:00:31.627
Phone digits can provide useful secondary evidence, but households may share a number and phone numbers can be

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:33.880
reassigned.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:40.977
Combining several fields usually gives stronger evidence, but it also makes the logic more complex.

00:00:40.977 --> 00:00:44.727
There is no perfect duplicate key for every dataset.

00:00:44.727 --> 00:00:51.652
Choose identity evidence based on the domain, understand the risks of false matches and missed matches,

00:00:51.652 --> 00:00:56.468
and use human review when the consequences of an incorrect merge are important.
